; E170 lane E -- MUST NOT COMPILE. Secret: the sealed handle sent on the wire.
;
; The wrong implementation: send the Secret itself. lib/secret.chiral:47-51 makes
; the claim in prose -- "the leak `(sock-send s pw)` does NOT type-check" -- and
; a claim in a comment is not evidence (test-floor.chiral:516-524). This file is
; the evidence, and it is the CUSTODY port's whole argument: a sealed value has
; exactly one exit, `secret-reveal`, and every other route out is closed by the
; type rather than by a convention.
;
; ⚑ WHY THE WRONGNESS IS SEMANTIC AND NOT STRUCTURAL. The twin `pe-secret-out`
; in e170_port_twin.chiral has the same declared type, the same two linear
; parameters, and reaches the same `sock-send`; it differs by taking the audited
; exit first. And the refusal is not an arity or a name: `Secret` is an opaque
; linear atom and `Bytes` is `sock-send`'s declared payload slot, so what
; refuses this is the port's nominal identity standing between a sealed value
; and a sink.
;
; ⚑ The honest limit, which secret.chiral states and this fixture does not
; enlarge: once REVEALED the bytes are ordinary and untracked. The twin sends
; them. What is refused is sending the handle, not leaking the plaintext --
; information-flow labelling is E44 and unbuilt.
;
; want: exit 1, `load: type mismatch`

(import "capability/secret")

(def pe-secret-out (=> (1 pw Secret) (1 s Sock) SendR)
  (lam (pw s) (sock-send s pw)))

(def compile-main (-> I64 I64) (lam (n) 0))
